;; A semicolon starts a comment

;; ---
;; 1. Primitive types, definitions and collections
;; ---

;; Numbers can be fixnums (integers)
123

;; Or flonums (floating point numbers)
123.4

;; Strings are about like you'd expect
"hello world"

;; Otium also supports Symbols.
;; Unlike strings, symbols are immutable and global. They're cheap to pass
;; around and good to use as keys for hash tables, arguments 
:my-symbol

;; We can call functions like this
(+ 2 2)

;; And we can define variables and functions using the `def` primitive
(def four (+ 2 2))

;; One thing to note is that Otium is a lot more permissive about what can go
;; into an identifier name than most languages
(def %f-o-u-r four)

;; Otium has a few different collection types.
;; There are lists
(list 1 2 3)

;; And arrays
(array 1 2 3)

;; And hash tables
(table :key 5)

;; Arrays and tables have literal syntaxes
[1 2 3]
{:key 5}

;; Lists need to be quoted to make them on the fly, because otherwise that would conflict with function application:
'(1 2 3)

;; Quoting is also important, but we'll discuss it in detail in the macros section.

